## Deploy Workflow (Trust-Safe)

```bash
# 1. Push code
git add -A
git commit -m "..."
git push origin main

# 2. Deploy
ssh root@68.183.168.75 'cd /opt/clinical-sovereignty-lab && \
  git pull origin main && \
  docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml build backend bridge && \
  docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d backend bridge'

# 3. Wait 5 minutes for auditor cycle
sleep 300

# 4. Verify
ssh root@68.183.168.75 'docker exec nate_postgres psql -U nate_admin -d little_nate -c \
  "SELECT parameter_key, parameter_value->>'\''score'\'' AS score, \
          parameter_value->>'\''expected'\'' AS expected, \
          parameter_value->>'\''trusted'\'' AS trusted \
   FROM trust_baseline ORDER BY parameter_key;"'

# 5. If any auditor < 97%, do not close the deploy task. Fix it.
```

## Audit Token Identity (Reference)

The audit Bearer token is the value of `SKYEYE_AUDIT_TOKEN` in `.env`.
It is registered in Redis at `nate:production:auth:{TOKEN_VALUE}` with payload:

```json
{
  "user_id": "AUDIT_SYSTEM",
  "id": "AUDIT_SYSTEM",
  "hardware_id": "AUDIT_SYSTEM",
  "username": "audit_system",
  "name": "Audit System",
  "role": "ADMIN",
  "tier": "TOP_TIER",
  "is_audit": true,
  "is_audit_token": true
}
```

The same token bypasses HTTP auth via `get_current_user` short-circuit
in `backend/app/services/api_server.py`. Both the WebSocket bridge and the
HTTP backend resolve the same audit identity.
